    user_response: "# Project Prompt \342\200\224 Digital Saathi\n\nCreate a **simple, modern, responsive educational website** called **\342\200\234Digital Saathi\342\200\235** for a **3rd-year BSc Computer Science CEP project** based on the theme:\n\n> **\342\200\234Building Open-Source Solutions for Social Problems\342\200\235**\n\n## 1. Project Concept\n\nDigital Saathi is a **digital literacy awareness website** designed to help people who are not comfortable with technology understand and safely use common digital services.\n\nThe website should **educate and guide users**, not function as a full-fledged application.\n\nThe target audience includes:\n\n* Elderly people\n* First-time smartphone users\n* People with limited digital knowledge\n* People unfamiliar with online payments\n* People who are vulnerable to online scams\n* People who need help understanding common digital services\n\nThe website should use **very simple language, large readable text, clear icons, visual explanations, and straightforward navigation**.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Important Scope Constraint\n\nThis is a **small academic CEP project**, NOT a commercial SaaS product.\n\nDo NOT add unnecessary complexity.\n\n### Do NOT include:\n\n* User registration/login\n* Backend\n* Database\n* Payment integration\n* Admin dashboard\n* Complex AI chatbot\n* Machine learning\n* Recommendation engine\n* Real-time communication\n* Complex authentication\n* User profiles\n\nThe website should primarily use:\n\n**HTML + CSS + JavaScript**\n\nIf a framework is used, keep the implementation lightweight.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Website Identity\n\n### Name\n\n**Digital Saathi**\n\n### Tagline\n\n**\342\200\234Making Technology Easier for Everyone.\342\200\235**\n\n### Secondary message\n\n**Learn. Understand. Stay Safe.**\n\nThe visual identity should feel:\n\n* Friendly\n* Trustworthy\n* Accessible\n* Educational\n* Modern\n* Clean\n* Non-technical\n\nAvoid making it look like a corporate banking website or a complicated technology dashboard.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Main Navigation\n\nCreate a responsive navigation bar containing:\n\n* Home\n* Digital Basics\n* Digital Payments\n* Online Safety\n* Digital Services\n* Learn & Test\n* About\n\nOn mobile, convert the navigation into a hamburger menu.\n\nInclude a clear **\342\200\234Start Learning\342\200\235** CTA.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Homepage\n\nCreate a visually strong but simple homepage.\n\n### Hero Section\n\nHeading:\n\n> **Digital Saathi**\n\nSubheading:\n\n> **Making Technology Easier for Everyone.**\n\nSupporting text:\n\n> Learn how to use everyday digital services, make safer online payments, identify scams, and confidently navigate the digital world.\n\nButtons:\n\n**Start Learning**\n\n**Stay Safe Online**\n\nInclude a friendly illustration representing digital literacy, smartphones, online services, or community learning.\n\n---\n\n## Homepage Sections\n\n### A. Why Digital Literacy Matters\n\nExplain briefly:\n\n> Technology is becoming part of everyday life, but not everyone has the knowledge or confidence to use it safely. Digital Saathi provides simple, practical guidance for common digital tasks.\n\nUse 3\342\200\2234 statistic-style cards or informational cards, but **do not invent statistics**.\n\nExample:\n\n* Digital Payments\n* Online Safety\n* Digital Services\n* Smartphone Skills\n\n---\n\n### B. Explore Digital Skills\n\nCreate four large cards:\n\n#### \360\237\223\261 Digital Basics\n\nLearn smartphone, internet, app and browser basics.\n\n#### \360\237\222\263 Digital Payments\n\nUnderstand UPI, QR payments and safe digital transactions.\n\n#### \360\237\233\241\357\270\217 Online Safety\n\nLearn how to identify scams, phishing and suspicious messages.\n\n#### \360\237\217\233\357\270\217 Digital Services\n\nUnderstand how to use common online government and public services.\n\nEach card should have a **Learn More** button.\n\n---\n\n### C. \342\200\234Can You Spot the Scam?\342\200\235\n\nCreate a highlighted interactive section.\n\nShow a fake-looking but clearly simulated message such as:\n\n> \342\200\234URGENT! Your bank account will be blocked today. Click this link to complete your KYC immediately.\342\200\235\n\nAsk:\n\n> **Is this Safe or a Scam?**\n\nButtons:\n\n**Safe**\n\n**Scam**\n\nWhen the user selects an answer, JavaScript should display feedback.\n\nFor the scam example, explain:\n\n* Creates urgency\n* Uses fear\n* Contains a suspicious link\n* Requests immediate action\n* Users should verify through official channels\n\nClearly label all examples as **simulated educational examples**.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Digital Basics Page\n\nCreate a simple educational page covering:\n\n### Smartphone Basics\n\n* Understanding the home screen\n* Opening and closing apps\n* Adjusting volume\n* Connecting to Wi-Fi\n* Managing mobile data\n\n### Internet Basics\n\n* What is the internet?\n* What is a browser?\n* How to search safely\n* Understanding website addresses\n\n### Apps\n\n* Installing apps\n* Updating apps\n* Checking app permissions\n* Removing unnecessary apps\n\nUse visual cards and step-by-step instructions.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Digital Payments Page\n\nCreate an educational page explaining:\n\n### What is UPI?\n\nExplain UPI in extremely simple language.\n\n### How to Make a UPI Payment\n\nCreate a visual step-by-step process:\n\n1. Open your UPI app\n2. Select the payment option\n3. Scan the QR code or enter the recipient\n4. Enter the amount\n5. Verify the recipient\n6. Enter your UPI PIN\n7. Confirm the transaction\n\n### Important Safety Rules\n\nHighlight:\n\n> **Never share your UPI PIN.**\n\n> **Never share your OTP.**\n\n> **Always verify the recipient before paying.**\n\n> **Receiving money does NOT require entering your UPI PIN.**\n\nInclude a warning section explaining common UPI scams.\n\nDo not connect to real banking systems.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Online Safety Page\n\nCreate a dedicated online safety section.\n\nTopics:\n\n### Phishing\n\nExplain what phishing means in simple language.\n\n### OTP Scams\n\nExplain why OTPs should never be shared.\n\n### Fake Links\n\nShow examples of suspicious URLs and explain warning signs.\n\n### Fake Customer Care\n\nExplain how scammers impersonate banks, companies and services.\n\n### Fake Job Offers\n\nExplain common warning signs.\n\n### Social Engineering\n\nExplain how scammers manipulate people into revealing information.\n\nUse:\n\n**DO / DON\'T**\n\ncomparison cards.\n\nExample:\n\n### DO\n\n* Verify the sender\n* Check the official website\n* Think before clicking\n* Contact official support\n\n### DON\'T\n\n* Share OTPs\n* Share passwords\n* Share UPI PINs\n* Click unknown links\n* Trust urgent messages blindly\n\n---\n\n# 9. Digital Services Page\n\nIntroduce common digital public services.\n\nCreate cards for:\n\n### DigiLocker\n\nExplain what it is and how digital documents can be accessed.\n\n### Online Government Services\n\nExplain how users can safely find and use official government websites.\n\n### Online Forms\n\nExplain basic precautions when filling out online forms.\n\n### Digital Documents\n\nExplain safe handling and sharing of digital documents.\n\nImportant:\n\nDo not create fake government services.\n\nWhere appropriate, provide links to official websites.\n\nClearly distinguish between **Digital Saathi\'s educational content** and external official services.\n\n---\n\n# 10. Learn & Test Page\n\nCreate a small interactive quiz using JavaScript.\n\nInclude approximately **8\342\200\22310 questions**.\n\nExample:\n\n### Question 1\n\nSomeone asks you for your UPI PIN to receive money.\n\nWhat should you do?\n\nA. Give it\nB. Share only half\nC. Never share it\nD. Send your OTP instead\n\nCorrect answer:\n\n**C. Never share it**\n\nAfter each answer:\n\n* Show whether it is correct\n* Explain why\n* Move to the next question\n\nAt the end show:\n\n> **Your Score: 8/10**\n\nGive simple feedback such as:\n\n* 8\342\200\22310: Great digital awareness\n* 5\342\200\2237: Good start \342\200\224 review a few topics\n* 0\342\200\2234: Go through the safety lessons again\n\nDo not store scores permanently.\n\n---\n\n# 11. About Page\n\nExplain the project:\n\n### What is Digital Saathi?\n\nDigital Saathi is an **open-source educational initiative** created as part of a college Community Engagement Project.\n\n### Social Problem\n\nExplain the digital literacy gap and how lack of digital knowledge can lead to:\n\n* Difficulty accessing services\n* Dependence on others\n* Online fraud\n* Unsafe digital practices\n* Lack of confidence with technology\n\n### Our Goal\n\n> Make basic digital knowledge simple, accessible and understandable for everyone.\n\n### Open Source\n\nExplain that the project can be improved and adapted by others.\n\nPotential future contributors can add:\n\n* New languages\n* New learning material\n* New scam examples\n* New quizzes\n* Accessibility improvements\n\nInclude a placeholder:\n\n**View Project on GitHub**\n\nUse a clearly marked placeholder URL if an actual repository does not exist yet.\n\n---\n\n# 12. Accessibility\n\nAccessibility is extremely important because the target audience includes people with limited digital literacy.\n\nImplement:\n\n* Large readable typography\n* High contrast\n* Clear buttons\n* Simple language\n* Descriptive icons\n* Adequate spacing\n* Keyboard navigation\n* Visible focus states\n* Responsive design\n* Alt text for meaningful images\n* Avoid excessive animations\n* Avoid tiny text\n\nAdd a simple **text-size control** if practical:\n\n**A\342\210\222 | A | A+**\n\nUse JavaScript to increase/decrease the website\'s text size.\n\n---\n\n# 13. Language Selector\n\nAdd a language selector in the navigation.\n\nInitially provide:\n\n* English\n* Hindi\n* Marathi\n\nFor the prototype, the language selector can demonstrate the concept using a limited set of translated interface elements/content.\n\nDo not attempt to translate every page if it unnecessarily increases the project scope.\n\n---\n\n# 14. Design Direction\n\nUse a modern educational design.\n\n### Visual style\n\n* Clean white/light background\n* Soft rounded cards\n* Large typography\n* Friendly illustrations\n* Clear icons\n* Plenty of whitespace\n* Subtle shadows\n* Minimal animations\n\nUse a consistent color system that communicates:\n\n**Trust + Safety + Education**\n\nDo not make the interface overly colorful or childish.\n\n---\n\n# 15. Responsive Design\n\nThe website must work properly on:\n\n* Desktop\n* Laptop\n* Tablet\n* Mobile\n\nPay particular attention to mobile because the target audience will primarily access digital services through smartphones.\n\n---\n\n# 16. Interactive JavaScript Features\n\nAt minimum implement:\n\n### 1. Scam Detector\n\nUser chooses:\n\n**Safe / Scam**\n\nThen receives an explanation.\n\n### 2. Quiz\n\n8\342\200\22310 questions with score calculation.\n\n### 3. Text Size Control\n\nA\342\210\222 / A / A+\n\n### 4. Mobile Navigation\n\nResponsive hamburger menu.\n\nOptional:\n\n### 5. Search\n\nAllow users to search topics such as:\n\n\342\200\234UPI\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234OTP\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234Phishing\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234DigiLocker\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234Wi-Fi\342\200\235\n\nIf implemented, keep it simple and client-side.\n\n---\n\n# 17. Footer\n\nInclude:\n\n**Digital Saathi**\n\n> Making Technology Easier for Everyone.\n\nFooter links:\n\n* Home\n* Learn\n* Online Safety\n* Quiz\n* About\n* GitHub\n\nInclude:\n\n> **Open Source Educational Project**\n\nAnd:\n\n> \302\251 2026 Digital Saathi\n\n---\n\n# 18. Content Guidelines\n\nThe content must be:\n\n* Simple\n* Practical\n* Factually accurate\n* Beginner-friendly\n* Short and scannable\n\nAvoid technical jargon.\n\nInstead of:\n\n> \342\200\234Two-factor authentication provides an additional authentication layer\342\200\246\342\200\235\n\nsay:\n\n> **\342\200\234Two-factor authentication adds an extra security step when you sign in.\342\200\235**\n\nUse examples that are relevant to India.\n\nDo not make unsupported claims or invent statistics.\n\n---\n\n# 19. Project Structure\n\nOrganize the code cleanly.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ndigital-saathi/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 index.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 basics.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 payments.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 safety.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 quiz.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 about.html\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 css/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 style.css\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 js/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 main.js\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 quiz.js\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 accessibility.js\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 assets/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 images/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 icons/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 README.md\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 LICENSE\n```\n\n---\n\n# 20. Open-Source Requirements\n\nInclude a professional README containing:\n\n* Project name\n* Problem statement\n* Project objective\n* Features\n* Technology used\n* How to run locally\n* Screenshots section\n* Contribution guidelines\n* Future improvements\n* License\n\nUse an open-source license such as **MIT License** unless the college specifies another license.\n\n---\n\n# 21. Overall Goal\n\nThe final website should feel like a **real public-awareness initiative**, not a college template website.\n\nThe project should communicate:\n\n> **\342\200\234Technology is useful only when people know how to use it safely.\342\200\235**\n\nKeep the implementation intentionally simple, but make the **UI, content quality, accessibility, and educational experience polished**.\n\nThe final result should be suitable for:\n\n* CEP demonstration\n* College presentation\n* Project report\n* GitHub repository\n* Basic community awareness use\n\nDo not overengineer the project. Prioritize **clarity, usability, social relevance and polished execution** over technical complexity.\n\n"
    items {
      text: "# Project Prompt \342\200\224 Digital Saathi\n\nCreate a **simple, modern, responsive educational website** called **\342\200\234Digital Saathi\342\200\235** for a **3rd-year BSc Computer Science CEP project** based on the theme:\n\n> **\342\200\234Building Open-Source Solutions for Social Problems\342\200\235**\n\n## 1. Project Concept\n\nDigital Saathi is a **digital literacy awareness website** designed to help people who are not comfortable with technology understand and safely use common digital services.\n\nThe website should **educate and guide users**, not function as a full-fledged application.\n\nThe target audience includes:\n\n* Elderly people\n* First-time smartphone users\n* People with limited digital knowledge\n* People unfamiliar with online payments\n* People who are vulnerable to online scams\n* People who need help understanding common digital services\n\nThe website should use **very simple language, large readable text, clear icons, visual explanations, and straightforward navigation**.\n\n---\n\n# 2. Important Scope Constraint\n\nThis is a **small academic CEP project**, NOT a commercial SaaS product.\n\nDo NOT add unnecessary complexity.\n\n### Do NOT include:\n\n* User registration/login\n* Backend\n* Database\n* Payment integration\n* Admin dashboard\n* Complex AI chatbot\n* Machine learning\n* Recommendation engine\n* Real-time communication\n* Complex authentication\n* User profiles\n\nThe website should primarily use:\n\n**HTML + CSS + JavaScript**\n\nIf a framework is used, keep the implementation lightweight.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Website Identity\n\n### Name\n\n**Digital Saathi**\n\n### Tagline\n\n**\342\200\234Making Technology Easier for Everyone.\342\200\235**\n\n### Secondary message\n\n**Learn. Understand. Stay Safe.**\n\nThe visual identity should feel:\n\n* Friendly\n* Trustworthy\n* Accessible\n* Educational\n* Modern\n* Clean\n* Non-technical\n\nAvoid making it look like a corporate banking website or a complicated technology dashboard.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Main Navigation\n\nCreate a responsive navigation bar containing:\n\n* Home\n* Digital Basics\n* Digital Payments\n* Online Safety\n* Digital Services\n* Learn & Test\n* About\n\nOn mobile, convert the navigation into a hamburger menu.\n\nInclude a clear **\342\200\234Start Learning\342\200\235** CTA.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Homepage\n\nCreate a visually strong but simple homepage.\n\n### Hero Section\n\nHeading:\n\n> **Digital Saathi**\n\nSubheading:\n\n> **Making Technology Easier for Everyone.**\n\nSupporting text:\n\n> Learn how to use everyday digital services, make safer online payments, identify scams, and confidently navigate the digital world.\n\nButtons:\n\n**Start Learning**\n\n**Stay Safe Online**\n\nInclude a friendly illustration representing digital literacy, smartphones, online services, or community learning.\n\n---\n\n## Homepage Sections\n\n### A. Why Digital Literacy Matters\n\nExplain briefly:\n\n> Technology is becoming part of everyday life, but not everyone has the knowledge or confidence to use it safely. Digital Saathi provides simple, practical guidance for common digital tasks.\n\nUse 3\342\200\2234 statistic-style cards or informational cards, but **do not invent statistics**.\n\nExample:\n\n* Digital Payments\n* Online Safety\n* Digital Services\n* Smartphone Skills\n\n---\n\n### B. Explore Digital Skills\n\nCreate four large cards:\n\n#### \360\237\223\261 Digital Basics\n\nLearn smartphone, internet, app and browser basics.\n\n#### \360\237\222\263 Digital Payments\n\nUnderstand UPI, QR payments and safe digital transactions.\n\n#### \360\237\233\241\357\270\217 Online Safety\n\nLearn how to identify scams, phishing and suspicious messages.\n\n#### \360\237\217\233\357\270\217 Digital Services\n\nUnderstand how to use common online government and public services.\n\nEach card should have a **Learn More** button.\n\n---\n\n### C. \342\200\234Can You Spot the Scam?\342\200\235\n\nCreate a highlighted interactive section.\n\nShow a fake-looking but clearly simulated message such as:\n\n> \342\200\234URGENT! Your bank account will be blocked today. Click this link to complete your KYC immediately.\342\200\235\n\nAsk:\n\n> **Is this Safe or a Scam?**\n\nButtons:\n\n**Safe**\n\n**Scam**\n\nWhen the user selects an answer, JavaScript should display feedback.\n\nFor the scam example, explain:\n\n* Creates urgency\n* Uses fear\n* Contains a suspicious link\n* Requests immediate action\n* Users should verify through official channels\n\nClearly label all examples as **simulated educational examples**.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Digital Basics Page\n\nCreate a simple educational page covering:\n\n### Smartphone Basics\n\n* Understanding the home screen\n* Opening and closing apps\n* Adjusting volume\n* Connecting to Wi-Fi\n* Managing mobile data\n\n### Internet Basics\n\n* What is the internet?\n* What is a browser?\n* How to search safely\n* Understanding website addresses\n\n### Apps\n\n* Installing apps\n* Updating apps\n* Checking app permissions\n* Removing unnecessary apps\n\nUse visual cards and step-by-step instructions.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Digital Payments Page\n\nCreate an educational page explaining:\n\n### What is UPI?\n\nExplain UPI in extremely simple language.\n\n### How to Make a UPI Payment\n\nCreate a visual step-by-step process:\n\n1. Open your UPI app\n2. Select the payment option\n3. Scan the QR code or enter the recipient\n4. Enter the amount\n5. Verify the recipient\n6. Enter your UPI PIN\n7. Confirm the transaction\n\n### Important Safety Rules\n\nHighlight:\n\n> **Never share your UPI PIN.**\n\n> **Never share your OTP.**\n\n> **Always verify the recipient before paying.**\n\n> **Receiving money does NOT require entering your UPI PIN.**\n\nInclude a warning section explaining common UPI scams.\n\nDo not connect to real banking systems.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Online Safety Page\n\nCreate a dedicated online safety section.\n\nTopics:\n\n### Phishing\n\nExplain what phishing means in simple language.\n\n### OTP Scams\n\nExplain why OTPs should never be shared.\n\n### Fake Links\n\nShow examples of suspicious URLs and explain warning signs.\n\n### Fake Customer Care\n\nExplain how scammers impersonate banks, companies and services.\n\n### Fake Job Offers\n\nExplain common warning signs.\n\n### Social Engineering\n\nExplain how scammers manipulate people into revealing information.\n\nUse:\n\n**DO / DON\'T**\n\ncomparison cards.\n\nExample:\n\n### DO\n\n* Verify the sender\n* Check the official website\n* Think before clicking\n* Contact official support\n\n### DON\'T\n\n* Share OTPs\n* Share passwords\n* Share UPI PINs\n* Click unknown links\n* Trust urgent messages blindly\n\n---\n\n# 9. Digital Services Page\n\nIntroduce common digital public services.\n\nCreate cards for:\n\n### DigiLocker\n\nExplain what it is and how digital documents can be accessed.\n\n### Online Government Services\n\nExplain how users can safely find and use official government websites.\n\n### Online Forms\n\nExplain basic precautions when filling out online forms.\n\n### Digital Documents\n\nExplain safe handling and sharing of digital documents.\n\nImportant:\n\nDo not create fake government services.\n\nWhere appropriate, provide links to official websites.\n\nClearly distinguish between **Digital Saathi\'s educational content** and external official services.\n\n---\n\n# 10. Learn & Test Page\n\nCreate a small interactive quiz using JavaScript.\n\nInclude approximately **8\342\200\22310 questions**.\n\nExample:\n\n### Question 1\n\nSomeone asks you for your UPI PIN to receive money.\n\nWhat should you do?\n\nA. Give it\nB. Share only half\nC. Never share it\nD. Send your OTP instead\n\nCorrect answer:\n\n**C. Never share it**\n\nAfter each answer:\n\n* Show whether it is correct\n* Explain why\n* Move to the next question\n\nAt the end show:\n\n> **Your Score: 8/10**\n\nGive simple feedback such as:\n\n* 8\342\200\22310: Great digital awareness\n* 5\342\200\2237: Good start \342\200\224 review a few topics\n* 0\342\200\2234: Go through the safety lessons again\n\nDo not store scores permanently.\n\n---\n\n# 11. About Page\n\nExplain the project:\n\n### What is Digital Saathi?\n\nDigital Saathi is an **open-source educational initiative** created as part of a college Community Engagement Project.\n\n### Social Problem\n\nExplain the digital literacy gap and how lack of digital knowledge can lead to:\n\n* Difficulty accessing services\n* Dependence on others\n* Online fraud\n* Unsafe digital practices\n* Lack of confidence with technology\n\n### Our Goal\n\n> Make basic digital knowledge simple, accessible and understandable for everyone.\n\n### Open Source\n\nExplain that the project can be improved and adapted by others.\n\nPotential future contributors can add:\n\n* New languages\n* New learning material\n* New scam examples\n* New quizzes\n* Accessibility improvements\n\nInclude a placeholder:\n\n**View Project on GitHub**\n\nUse a clearly marked placeholder URL if an actual repository does not exist yet.\n\n---\n\n# 12. Accessibility\n\nAccessibility is extremely important because the target audience includes people with limited digital literacy.\n\nImplement:\n\n* Large readable typography\n* High contrast\n* Clear buttons\n* Simple language\n* Descriptive icons\n* Adequate spacing\n* Keyboard navigation\n* Visible focus states\n* Responsive design\n* Alt text for meaningful images\n* Avoid excessive animations\n* Avoid tiny text\n\nAdd a simple **text-size control** if practical:\n\n**A\342\210\222 | A | A+**\n\nUse JavaScript to increase/decrease the website\'s text size.\n\n---\n\n# 13. Language Selector\n\nAdd a language selector in the navigation.\n\nInitially provide:\n\n* English\n* Hindi\n* Marathi\n\nFor the prototype, the language selector can demonstrate the concept using a limited set of translated interface elements/content.\n\nDo not attempt to translate every page if it unnecessarily increases the project scope.\n\n---\n\n# 14. Design Direction\n\nUse a modern educational design.\n\n### Visual style\n\n* Clean white/light background\n* Soft rounded cards\n* Large typography\n* Friendly illustrations\n* Clear icons\n* Plenty of whitespace\n* Subtle shadows\n* Minimal animations\n\nUse a consistent color system that communicates:\n\n**Trust + Safety + Education**\n\nDo not make the interface overly colorful or childish.\n\n---\n\n# 15. Responsive Design\n\nThe website must work properly on:\n\n* Desktop\n* Laptop\n* Tablet\n* Mobile\n\nPay particular attention to mobile because the target audience will primarily access digital services through smartphones.\n\n---\n\n# 16. Interactive JavaScript Features\n\nAt minimum implement:\n\n### 1. Scam Detector\n\nUser chooses:\n\n**Safe / Scam**\n\nThen receives an explanation.\n\n### 2. Quiz\n\n8\342\200\22310 questions with score calculation.\n\n### 3. Text Size Control\n\nA\342\210\222 / A / A+\n\n### 4. Mobile Navigation\n\nResponsive hamburger menu.\n\nOptional:\n\n### 5. Search\n\nAllow users to search topics such as:\n\n\342\200\234UPI\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234OTP\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234Phishing\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234DigiLocker\342\200\235\n\n\342\200\234Wi-Fi\342\200\235\n\nIf implemented, keep it simple and client-side.\n\n---\n\n# 17. Footer\n\nInclude:\n\n**Digital Saathi**\n\n> Making Technology Easier for Everyone.\n\nFooter links:\n\n* Home\n* Learn\n* Online Safety\n* Quiz\n* About\n* GitHub\n\nInclude:\n\n> **Open Source Educational Project**\n\nAnd:\n\n> \302\251 2026 Digital Saathi\n\n---\n\n# 18. Content Guidelines\n\nThe content must be:\n\n* Simple\n* Practical\n* Factually accurate\n* Beginner-friendly\n* Short and scannable\n\nAvoid technical jargon.\n\nInstead of:\n\n> \342\200\234Two-factor authentication provides an additional authentication layer\342\200\246\342\200\235\n\nsay:\n\n> **\342\200\234Two-factor authentication adds an extra security step when you sign in.\342\200\235**\n\nUse examples that are relevant to India.\n\nDo not make unsupported claims or invent statistics.\n\n---\n\n# 19. Project Structure\n\nOrganize the code cleanly.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ndigital-saathi/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 index.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 basics.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 payments.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 safety.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 quiz.html\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 about.html\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 css/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 style.css\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 js/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 main.js\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 quiz.js\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 accessibility.js\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 assets/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 images/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 icons/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 README.md\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 LICENSE\n```\n\n---\n\n# 20. Open-Source Requirements\n\nInclude a professional README containing:\n\n* Project name\n* Problem statement\n* Project objective\n* Features\n* Technology used\n* How to run locally\n* Screenshots section\n* Contribution guidelines\n* Future improvements\n* License\n\nUse an open-source license such as **MIT License** unless the college specifies another license.\n\n---\n\n# 21. Overall Goal\n\nThe final website should feel like a **real public-awareness initiative**, not a college template website.\n\nThe project should communicate:\n\n> **\342\200\234Technology is useful only when people know how to use it safely.\342\200\235**\n\nKeep the implementation intentionally simple, but make the **UI, content quality, accessibility, and educational experience polished**.\n\nThe final result should be suitable for:\n\n* CEP demonstration\n* College presentation\n* Project report\n* GitHub repository\n* Basic community awareness use\n\nDo not overengineer the project. Prioritize **clarity, usability, social relevance and polished execution** over technical complexity.\n\n"
