    thinking: "**Defining Localization Needs**\n\nI\'m focusing now on the critical aspect of full-application text localization for Hindi and Marathi. The goal is seamless, near-total language conversion when the user switches languages, ensuring a consistent user experience in the selected language. This involves more than just a few strings, it\'s about a complete linguistic adaptation across the entire application.\n\n\n**Structuring Localization Components**\n\nI\'ve moved towards a structured approach, enhancing existing types and creating comprehensive data files. Specifically, I\'m working to expand `src/types.ts` to include localized fields in `ScamScenario` and `QuizQuestion`. Concurrently, I\'m generating `translations.ts` and `scamScenarios.ts`, and `quizQuestions.ts` with complete content for Hindi and Marathi, including rich translations for shared strings and all scenarios. Now, the main task is to organize page content files or structured localized data objects.\n\n\n**Mapping Content for Translation**\n\nI\'m now identifying every text element within each page component: `HomePage.tsx`, `BasicsPage.tsx`, `PaymentsPage.tsx`, and so on. This encompasses everything \342\200\223 hero badges, checklists, step guides, and even micro-copy like taglines. The goal is to create a complete inventory of content that needs Hindi and Marathi translations across the application. I\'m focusing on a detailed component-by-component breakdown for comprehensive coverage.\n\n\n"
