steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1787918337
      nanos: 258294739
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "REDESIGN ONLY THE UI/UX OF THE EXISTING DIGITAL SAATHI WEBSITE.\n\nDO NOT change the existing features, functionality, content, routes, or project concept.\nDO NOT add major new features.\nFocus entirely on improving the frontend design and user experience.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nPRIMARY TARGET AUDIENCE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nThe website is NOT designed primarily for Gen-Z.\n\nThe primary users are:\n\n- Older adults\n- Parents and grandparents\n- First-time smartphone users\n- People with limited digital literacy\n- People who are uncomfortable with online payments\n- People who may be vulnerable to online scams\n- Users who prefer simple, familiar interfaces\n\nThe design must therefore prioritize:\n\nCLARITY\nREADABILITY\nTRUST\nSIMPLICITY\nACCESSIBILITY\nCONFIDENCE\nEASE OF USE\n\nThe website should feel modern and professionally designed, but never trendy at the expense of usability.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nOVERALL DESIGN DIRECTION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nCreate a:\n\nMODERN + MINIMAL + HUMAN + ACCESSIBLE + TRUSTWORTHY\n\ndigital literacy website.\n\nThink of the visual quality of a modern technology product, but with the simplicity of a well-designed public service website.\n\nIt should feel calm and reassuring.\n\nNOT:\n- Gen-Z aesthetic\n- trendy startup\n- AI-generated SaaS\n- fintech dashboard\n- luxury/caf\303\251 brand\n- futuristic interface\n- government portal from the early 2010s\n\nThe design should feel timeless rather than fashionable.\n\nA user should be able to visit the website and immediately feel:\n\n\"I understand what this website does.\"\n\"I know where to click.\"\n\"I won\'t get confused here.\"\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nVISUAL LANGUAGE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nUse a restrained, minimal visual system.\n\nBase:\n- warm white / off-white background\n- dark charcoal text\n- subtle gray borders\n- one calm accent color\n\nPossible accent colors:\n- muted blue\n- calm green\n- deep teal\n\nChoose ONE primary accent and use it consistently.\n\nAvoid:\n- gradients\n- neon colors\n- excessive color coding\n- glassmorphism\n- excessive shadows\n- glowing effects\n- decorative blobs\n- unnecessary visual effects\n\nUse color mainly to communicate:\n- actions\n- active states\n- success\n- warnings\n- important information\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nTYPOGRAPHY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nTypography is extremely important.\n\nUse a highly readable modern sans-serif such as:\n\nInter\nManrope\nDM Sans\nSource Sans 3\nPlus Jakarta Sans\n\nPrioritize readability over visual experimentation.\n\nUse:\n- large headings\n- comfortable body text\n- generous line height\n- clear hierarchy\n- medium/bold weights for important information\n\nDO NOT use overly thin fonts.\n\nDO NOT use extremely condensed fonts.\n\nDO NOT rely heavily on italic serif typography.\n\nText should remain readable for older users.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nSPACING\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nUse generous spacing.\n\nDo not overcrowd the interface.\n\nEvery section should have clear separation.\n\nUse consistent spacing between:\n- headings\n- descriptions\n- buttons\n- content blocks\n- navigation elements\n\nThe interface should breathe.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nNAVIGATION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nKeep navigation extremely simple.\n\nPrioritize recognition over compactness.\n\nUse clear labels such as:\n\nHome\nLearn\nSafety\nServices\nQuiz\nAbout\n\nAvoid complicated terminology.\n\nKeep:\n- Search\n- Language selector\n- Text-size controls\n- Start Learning\n\nBut make them visually simple and easy to understand.\n\nDo not hide important navigation behind unnecessary interactions on desktop.\n\nOn mobile, use a clear hamburger menu with large touch targets.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nACCESSIBILITY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nAccessibility is a CORE design requirement, not an optional feature.\n\nUse:\n\n- minimum comfortable body text size\n- strong text/background contrast\n- large buttons\n- large click/tap targets\n- clear focus states\n- readable line spacing\n- simple language\n- descriptive labels\n- obvious icons\n- no tiny text\n- no overly subtle UI controls\n\nKeep the existing:\n\nA-\nA\nA+\n\ntext-size controls and make them easy to discover.\n\nThe interface should work comfortably for someone who may have:\n- weaker eyesight\n- limited technology experience\n- difficulty understanding technical terminology\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nHERO SECTION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nKeep the existing hero structure but make it more welcoming.\n\nHeadline:\n\n\"Making technology\neasier & safer for everyone.\"\n\nUse large, highly readable typography.\n\nSupporting text should clearly explain the website in simple language.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Learn how to use everyday digital services, make safer payments, recognise online scams, and use technology with confidence.\"\n\nPrimary CTA:\n\nStart Learning \342\206\222\n\nSecondary CTA:\n\nStay Safe Online\n\nAdd a small reassuring message:\n\n\"Simple guides. Clear steps. No technical knowledge required.\"\n\nAvoid excessive decorative elements.\n\nThe hero should have one strong visual rather than multiple floating cards.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nHOMEPAGE UX\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nImmediately after the hero, make the user\'s choices extremely obvious.\n\nUse:\n\n\"What would you like help with?\"\n\nThen provide four large, clearly labelled options:\n\n\360\237\223\261 Learn to use my phone\n\n\360\237\222\263 Understand digital payments\n\n\360\237\233\241\357\270\217 Stay safe from scams\n\n\360\237\223\204 Use online services\n\nThese should connect to the existing website sections.\n\nThe user should not have to understand technical categories.\n\nDesign around USER INTENT rather than technology terminology.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nCONTENT DESIGN\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nDo not put every piece of information inside a card.\n\nUse a mixture of:\n\n- clear headings\n- numbered steps\n- simple illustrations\n- short explanations\n- large icons\n- checklists\n- warning boxes\n- examples\n- simple comparison sections\n\nCards should only be used when they genuinely improve comprehension.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nDIGITAL BASICS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nMake instructions extremely visual and sequential.\n\nExample:\n\n01\nOpen your phone\n\n02\nFind the app you want\n\n03\nTap the app icon\n\n04\nFollow the instructions on screen\n\nUse large numbers and simple explanations.\n\nAvoid paragraphs of technical information.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nDIGITAL PAYMENTS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nMake payment education extremely clear.\n\nImportant information should be visually prominent:\n\nNEVER SHARE YOUR UPI PIN.\n\nNEVER SHARE YOUR OTP.\n\nALWAYS CHECK WHO YOU ARE PAYING.\n\nDo not bury safety warnings inside paragraphs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nONLINE SAFETY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nKeep the existing scam detector.\n\nMake it simple and realistic.\n\nSection title:\n\n\"Before You Click\"\n\nShow a simulated SMS/email/message.\n\nAsk:\n\n\"Could this be a scam?\"\n\nButtons:\n\n\"This looks safe\"\n\n\"This looks suspicious\"\n\nAfter selection, clearly explain why.\n\nAvoid making the interaction look like a game.\n\nThe purpose is education and confidence-building.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nQUIZ\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nKeep the existing quiz functionality.\n\nMake it extremely easy to understand.\n\nShow:\n\nQuestion 3 of 10\n\n[Question]\n\n[Answer option]\n\n[Answer option]\n\n[Answer option]\n\nNext \342\206\222\n\nUse clear feedback.\n\nDo not use complicated gamification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nICONS & IMAGERY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nUse familiar, universally understandable icons.\n\nExamples:\n\nphone\ncard\nshield\ndocument\nlock\nwarning\nsearch\narrow\n\nAvoid abstract icons that require interpretation.\n\nIf photography is used, prefer authentic everyday situations:\n\n- older adults using smartphones\n- families making digital payments\n- people learning technology\n- someone checking a suspicious message\n\nAvoid:\n- robots\n- futuristic interfaces\n- abstract 3D graphics\n- generic AI illustrations\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nMICRO-INTERACTIONS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nKeep interactions subtle.\n\nUse:\n- smooth hover states\n- clear button feedback\n- gentle transitions\n- simple quiz feedback\n- subtle section animations\n\nDo NOT use:\n- bouncing animations\n- excessive motion\n- animated gradients\n- glowing elements\n- distracting parallax\n\nOlder users should never feel that the interface is moving too much.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nLANGUAGE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nUse plain everyday language.\n\nAvoid terms such as:\n\n\"Digital ecosystem\"\n\"Cyber resilience\"\n\"Authentication layer\"\n\"Digital infrastructure\"\n\nPrefer:\n\n\"Stay safe online\"\n\"Keep your account safe\"\n\"Check before you click\"\n\"Learn step by step\"\n\nThe website should speak like a helpful person, not a technical manual.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nRESPONSIVE DESIGN\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nDesktop, tablet and mobile layouts must all remain accessible.\n\nMobile design is especially important because many users will access the website through smartphones.\n\nUse:\n- large tap targets\n- readable text\n- simple stacking\n- clear spacing\n- obvious navigation\n\nDo not simply shrink the desktop interface.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nFINAL DESIGN PRINCIPLE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nThe website should NOT try to impress users with visual complexity.\n\nIt should make users feel comfortable.\n\nThe ideal reaction is:\n\n\"I can understand this.\"\n\n\"I know what to do.\"\n\n\"I don\'t need to be good at technology.\"\n\n\"I feel safe using this website.\"\n\nMake Digital Saathi feel like a modern, polished, trustworthy digital literacy service designed specifically for people who need technology to be SIMPLE.\n\nModern, but not trendy.\nMinimal, but not empty.\nProfessional, but not corporate.\nFriendly, but not childish.\nAccessible, but not visually boring.\n\nPRIORITY ORDER:\n\n1. Usability\n2. Readability\n3. Accessibility\n4. Trust\n5. Clear information hierarchy\n6. Modern visual design\n7. Visual personality\n\nNever sacrifice the first five for aesthetics."
    items {
      text: "REDESIGN ONLY THE UI/UX OF THE EXISTING DIGITAL SAATHI WEBSITE.\n\nDO NOT change the existing features, functionality, content, routes, or project concept.\nDO NOT add major new features.\nFocus entirely on improving the frontend design and user experience.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nPRIMARY TARGET AUDIENCE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nThe website is NOT designed primarily for Gen-Z.\n\nThe primary users are:\n\n- Older adults\n- Parents and grandparents\n- First-time smartphone users\n- People with limited digital literacy\n- People who are uncomfortable with online payments\n- People who may be vulnerable to online scams\n- Users who prefer simple, familiar interfaces\n\nThe design must therefore prioritize:\n\nCLARITY\nREADABILITY\nTRUST\nSIMPLICITY\nACCESSIBILITY\nCONFIDENCE\nEASE OF USE\n\nThe website should feel modern and professionally designed, but never trendy at the expense of usability.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nOVERALL DESIGN DIRECTION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nCreate a:\n\nMODERN + MINIMAL + HUMAN + ACCESSIBLE + TRUSTWORTHY\n\ndigital literacy website.\n\nThink of the visual quality of a modern technology product, but with the simplicity of a well-designed public service website.\n\nIt should feel calm and reassuring.\n\nNOT:\n- Gen-Z aesthetic\n- trendy startup\n- AI-generated SaaS\n- fintech dashboard\n- luxury/caf\303\251 brand\n- futuristic interface\n- government portal from the early 2010s\n\nThe design should feel timeless rather than fashionable.\n\nA user should be able to visit the website and immediately feel:\n\n\"I understand what this website does.\"\n\"I know where to click.\"\n\"I won\'t get confused here.\"\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nVISUAL LANGUAGE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nUse a restrained, minimal visual system.\n\nBase:\n- warm white / off-white background\n- dark charcoal text\n- subtle gray borders\n- one calm accent color\n\nPossible accent colors:\n- muted blue\n- calm green\n- deep teal\n\nChoose ONE primary accent and use it consistently.\n\nAvoid:\n- gradients\n- neon colors\n- excessive color coding\n- glassmorphism\n- excessive shadows\n- glowing effects\n- decorative blobs\n- unnecessary visual effects\n\nUse color mainly to communicate:\n- actions\n- active states\n- success\n- warnings\n- important information\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nTYPOGRAPHY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nTypography is extremely important.\n\nUse a highly readable modern sans-serif such as:\n\nInter\nManrope\nDM Sans\nSource Sans 3\nPlus Jakarta Sans\n\nPrioritize readability over visual experimentation.\n\nUse:\n- large headings\n- comfortable body text\n- generous line height\n- clear hierarchy\n- medium/bold weights for important information\n\nDO NOT use overly thin fonts.\n\nDO NOT use extremely condensed fonts.\n\nDO NOT rely heavily on italic serif typography.\n\nText should remain readable for older users.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nSPACING\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nUse generous spacing.\n\nDo not overcrowd the interface.\n\nEvery section should have clear separation.\n\nUse consistent spacing between:\n- headings\n- descriptions\n- buttons\n- content blocks\n- navigation elements\n\nThe interface should breathe.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nNAVIGATION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nKeep navigation extremely simple.\n\nPrioritize recognition over compactness.\n\nUse clear labels such as:\n\nHome\nLearn\nSafety\nServices\nQuiz\nAbout\n\nAvoid complicated terminology.\n\nKeep:\n- Search\n- Language selector\n- Text-size controls\n- Start Learning\n\nBut make them visually simple and easy to understand.\n\nDo not hide important navigation behind unnecessary interactions on desktop.\n\nOn mobile, use a clear hamburger menu with large touch targets.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nACCESSIBILITY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nAccessibility is a CORE design requirement, not an optional feature.\n\nUse:\n\n- minimum comfortable body text size\n- strong text/background contrast\n- large buttons\n- large click/tap targets\n- clear focus states\n- readable line spacing\n- simple language\n- descriptive labels\n- obvious icons\n- no tiny text\n- no overly subtle UI controls\n\nKeep the existing:\n\nA-\nA\nA+\n\ntext-size controls and make them easy to discover.\n\nThe interface should work comfortably for someone who may have:\n- weaker eyesight\n- limited technology experience\n- difficulty understanding technical terminology\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nHERO SECTION\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nKeep the existing hero structure but make it more welcoming.\n\nHeadline:\n\n\"Making technology\neasier & safer for everyone.\"\n\nUse large, highly readable typography.\n\nSupporting text should clearly explain the website in simple language.\n\nExample:\n\n\"Learn how to use everyday digital services, make safer payments, recognise online scams, and use technology with confidence.\"\n\nPrimary CTA:\n\nStart Learning \342\206\222\n\nSecondary CTA:\n\nStay Safe Online\n\nAdd a small reassuring message:\n\n\"Simple guides. Clear steps. No technical knowledge required.\"\n\nAvoid excessive decorative elements.\n\nThe hero should have one strong visual rather than multiple floating cards.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nHOMEPAGE UX\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nImmediately after the hero, make the user\'s choices extremely obvious.\n\nUse:\n\n\"What would you like help with?\"\n\nThen provide four large, clearly labelled options:\n\n\360\237\223\261 Learn to use my phone\n\n\360\237\222\263 Understand digital payments\n\n\360\237\233\241\357\270\217 Stay safe from scams\n\n\360\237\223\204 Use online services\n\nThese should connect to the existing website sections.\n\nThe user should not have to understand technical categories.\n\nDesign around USER INTENT rather than technology terminology.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nCONTENT DESIGN\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nDo not put every piece of information inside a card.\n\nUse a mixture of:\n\n- clear headings\n- numbered steps\n- simple illustrations\n- short explanations\n- large icons\n- checklists\n- warning boxes\n- examples\n- simple comparison sections\n\nCards should only be used when they genuinely improve comprehension.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nDIGITAL BASICS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nMake instructions extremely visual and sequential.\n\nExample:\n\n01\nOpen your phone\n\n02\nFind the app you want\n\n03\nTap the app icon\n\n04\nFollow the instructions on screen\n\nUse large numbers and simple explanations.\n\nAvoid paragraphs of technical information.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nDIGITAL PAYMENTS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nMake payment education extremely clear.\n\nImportant information should be visually prominent:\n\nNEVER SHARE YOUR UPI PIN.\n\nNEVER SHARE YOUR OTP.\n\nALWAYS CHECK WHO YOU ARE PAYING.\n\nDo not bury safety warnings inside paragraphs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nONLINE SAFETY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nKeep the existing scam detector.\n\nMake it simple and realistic.\n\nSection title:\n\n\"Before You Click\"\n\nShow a simulated SMS/email/message.\n\nAsk:\n\n\"Could this be a scam?\"\n\nButtons:\n\n\"This looks safe\"\n\n\"This looks suspicious\"\n\nAfter selection, clearly explain why.\n\nAvoid making the interaction look like a game.\n\nThe purpose is education and confidence-building.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nQUIZ\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nKeep the existing quiz functionality.\n\nMake it extremely easy to understand.\n\nShow:\n\nQuestion 3 of 10\n\n[Question]\n\n[Answer option]\n\n[Answer option]\n\n[Answer option]\n\nNext \342\206\222\n\nUse clear feedback.\n\nDo not use complicated gamification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nICONS & IMAGERY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nUse familiar, universally understandable icons.\n\nExamples:\n\nphone\ncard\nshield\ndocument\nlock\nwarning\nsearch\narrow\n\nAvoid abstract icons that require interpretation.\n\nIf photography is used, prefer authentic everyday situations:\n\n- older adults using smartphones\n- families making digital payments\n- people learning technology\n- someone checking a suspicious message\n\nAvoid:\n- robots\n- futuristic interfaces\n- abstract 3D graphics\n- generic AI illustrations\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nMICRO-INTERACTIONS\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nKeep interactions subtle.\n\nUse:\n- smooth hover states\n- clear button feedback\n- gentle transitions\n- simple quiz feedback\n- subtle section animations\n\nDo NOT use:\n- bouncing animations\n- excessive motion\n- animated gradients\n- glowing elements\n- distracting parallax\n\nOlder users should never feel that the interface is moving too much.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nLANGUAGE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nUse plain everyday language.\n\nAvoid terms such as:\n\n\"Digital ecosystem\"\n\"Cyber resilience\"\n\"Authentication layer\"\n\"Digital infrastructure\"\n\nPrefer:\n\n\"Stay safe online\"\n\"Keep your account safe\"\n\"Check before you click\"\n\"Learn step by step\"\n\nThe website should speak like a helpful person, not a technical manual.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nRESPONSIVE DESIGN\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nDesktop, tablet and mobile layouts must all remain accessible.\n\nMobile design is especially important because many users will access the website through smartphones.\n\nUse:\n- large tap targets\n- readable text\n- simple stacking\n- clear spacing\n- obvious navigation\n\nDo not simply shrink the desktop interface.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nFINAL DESIGN PRINCIPLE\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nThe website should NOT try to impress users with visual complexity.\n\nIt should make users feel comfortable.\n\nThe ideal reaction is:\n\n\"I can understand this.\"\n\n\"I know what to do.\"\n\n\"I don\'t need to be good at technology.\"\n\n\"I feel safe using this website.\"\n\nMake Digital Saathi feel like a modern, polished, trustworthy digital literacy service designed specifically for people who need technology to be SIMPLE.\n\nModern, but not trendy.\nMinimal, but not empty.\nProfessional, but not corporate.\nFriendly, but not childish.\nAccessible, but not visually boring.\n\nPRIORITY ORDER:\n\n1. Usability\n2. Readability\n3. Accessibility\n4. Trust\n5. Clear information hierarchy\n6. Modern visual design\n7. Visual personality\n\nNever sacrifice the first five for aesthetics."
    }
  }
}
